Question 2) "By identifying the vulnerable PHP script, security teams can directly address and mitigate the vulnerability. What's the complete URI of the PHP script vulnerable to XXE Injection?"
------------------

A simple:

http.request.uri == ".php"

Pulls the list of php requests made during the PCAP. Here we can see the attacker probing a whole host of random URI's, likely a dictionary attack. Following the dictionary attack, we see the attacker specifically target 
"/review/upload.php". The HTTP code 200 Okay is received. This URI has been identified as present. The attacker attempts a POST request which is successful.

Simultaneously the attacker is attempting an XXE injection. 


  <!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd">
]>
<foo>
    &xxe;
</foo>


As seen above, the attacker POSTs an in band XXE injection. The variable/Entity 'xxe' is defined as the contents of system file 'passwd'. 

Although a 200 reponse is returned, the file was not outputted. This does mean however that the XML parser is willing to parse the in band files.


