Question 7) "To eliminate the threat and prevent further unauthorized access, can you identify the name of the web shell that the attacker uploaded for remote code execution and persistence?"
------------

Earlier on in the challenge, I noticed some GET requests that had bash commands in the php parameters. I made a mental note of this as I felt it would become relevant. 

Knowing this, I filtered for all ".php" requests and located these requests again. They were bound for /uploads/booking.php.
Confused as to what "The name of the webshell" meant in the question, I simply just tried "booking", then "booking.php". The name the question was looking for is booking.php. 

The attacker had uploaded booking.php and was running "cmd" parameters locally on the machine from there.

